<h1>Quality Profiles</h1>
<p class="page-meta">
  A quality profile is the single most important config decision. Get this right first.
</p>

<h2>What a quality profile controls</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Allowed qualities</strong> — which resolutions and sources NGMS will accept</li>
  <li><strong>Quality order</strong> — priority when multiple acceptable releases are found simultaneously</li>
  <li><strong>Upgrade</strong> — whether NGMS replaces a grabbed file when a better one appears</li>
  <li><strong>Upgrade until</strong> — the target quality — once reached, no more upgrades</li>
  <li><strong>Minimum custom format score</strong> — optional lower bound on release scoring</li>
</ul>

<h2>Creating a profile</h2>

<div class="step">
  <div class="step-num">1</div>
  <div class="step-body">
    <h3>Settings → Quality Profiles → Add Profile</h3>
    <p>Give it a descriptive name like <strong>"1080p Bluray"</strong> or <strong>"Any"</strong>.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="step-num">2</div>
  <div class="step-body">
    <h3>Select allowed qualities</h3>
    <p>
      Check every quality tier you'll accept. Common choices: <strong>Bluray-1080p</strong>,
      <strong>WEB-1080p</strong>, <strong>Bluray-720p</strong>, <strong>WEB-720p</strong>.
      Order them by preference — drag to reorder.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="step-num">3</div>
  <div class="step-body">
    <h3>Set "Upgrade Until"</h3>
    <p>
      Choose the quality where upgrades stop. If you set <strong>Bluray-1080p</strong> as the
      ceiling, NGMS will upgrade from <strong>WEB-1080p</strong> when a Bluray rip appears, but
      won't upgrade again after that.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="step-num">4</div>
  <div class="step-body">
    <h3>Save and assign</h3>
    <p>
      The profile is now available when adding or editing any series or movie. You can also
      bulk-change profiles via the series or movie list view.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Recommended starter profiles</h2>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Profile name</th><th>Allowed</th><th>Upgrade until</th><th>Use case</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Any</strong></td>
      <td>All qualities</td>
      <td>Bluray-1080p</td>
      <td>Grab anything available, upgrade opportunistically</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>1080p</strong></td>
      <td>WEB-1080p, Bluray-1080p</td>
      <td>Bluray-1080p</td>
      <td>1080p only, no SD or 720p fallback</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>4K HDR</strong></td>
      <td>Remux-2160p, Bluray-2160p, WEB-2160p</td>
      <td>Remux-2160p</td>
      <td>4K only — requires 4K-capable display and storage</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>SD Only</strong></td>
      <td>SDTV, DVD</td>
      <td>DVD</td>
      <td>Low-bandwidth or legacy content</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Custom formats</h2>

<p>
  Custom formats let you score releases beyond resolution — by codec (<code>x265</code>,
  <code>AV1</code>), audio (<code>DTS-HD</code>, <code>TrueHD Atmos</code>), release group
  reputation, HDR type (<code>HDR10+</code>, <code>DV</code>), or any regex against the
  release name.
</p>
<p>
  Each custom format has a score. The total score of all matching formats is compared against
  the profile's <strong>minimum custom format score</strong>. Releases that fall below the
  minimum are rejected.
</p>
<p>
  Custom formats are defined in <strong>Settings → Custom Formats</strong> and then referenced
  inside quality profiles.
</p>

</DocsLayout>
